  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Chances for strong storms and flash flooding\ntoday and Monday.\n\nWNW flow remains over the Mid Atlantic region through Monday\nnight, then becomes more westerly and cyclonic for the remainder\nof the week as a mean trough digs south into the area. Within \nthis flow, a number of perturbations moving through will help \naugment an unstable air mass at the surface, triggering several \nrounds of convection. We have already seen the remnants of one \nMCS moving through parts of WV, VA, and NC this morning and \nafternoon. Storms were also being aided by a warm front\nsnaking through the forecast area. Additional showers and \nstorms may fire along the Blue Ridge and impact the Piedmont \nthis afternoon, however some of that area has been worked over \nfrom this morning's convection. So after a break for the \nmountains, another complex of storms is expected to dive towards\nWV and southwest VA after 7 PM through about midnight. This \ncluster was already developing in the OH Valley early this \nafternoon in association with an MCV.\n\nShear values will support clusters of multicells and perhaps a\nsupercell or two. Most concerning will be the potential for wet\nmicrobursts and flash flooding, with PWATs in the 1.8 to 2.2 \ninches range. We have already seen flash flooding today in \nGreenbrier and Summers Counties, and a Flood Watch has been\nissued for parts of SE WV and along the WV/VA border where FFG\nvalues are less than 1.50 inches for 1 hour. There is also an \nisolated tornado threat along the northward lifting warm front. \nThis includes SE WV and parts of SW and west central VA where \nthe supercell composite value hovers around 2 to 4 on the SPC \nmesoanalysis page, and where LCL heights are 500 to 750 meters \nAGL. \n\nA trailing cold front/frontal fracture pushes into the region\ntomorrow afternoon and evening, bringing additional showers and\nstorms to mainly the western mountains, although chances expand\nto the Piedmont by late afternoon into the evening. The severe\nweather threat should have shifted to be more across SW VA and \nNE NC by tomorrow as the cold front moves south. This front \nwill also usher in a drier airmass by Tuesday, which will be \nmaintained across the region through the middle of the week. \nRain chances don't look to return until Thursday, with the \npassage of another cold front.",
